integrated_rules:
Evaluate society-wide AI claims by asking which condition is reliably satisfied.

If (in most deployments) AI 
1) reliably improves broad everyday life outcomes—making daily life more enjoyable/convenient while improving health and the standard of living by helping people access useful information and services faster and more accurately; 
2) reliably makes work and learning easier for both students and professionals by automating or accelerating time-consuming activities (e.g., drafting, study support, problem-solving assistance) in a way that increases productivity and reduces avoidable effort rather than simply replacing all human roles; 
3) reliably increases accessibility for marginalized groups, especially people with disabilities, via adaptive interfaces and assistive capabilities that enable more communication, learning, and participation; and 
4) reliably improves workplace safety by monitoring conditions, flagging risks earlier, and supporting safer decision-making so accidents are less likely and/or less severe; 
AND these benefits are not undercut by (or are counterbalanced by) the following failure mode—namely that AI outputs are effectively a “black box” to ordinary people such that they cannot understand, verify, or effectively challenge decisions/recommendations, making the harms (undermined critical thinking; racial discrimination that is hard to detect/contest; privacy violations that are hard to prevent/reverse; and downstream economic insecurity/lock-in across employment and services) durable and not reliably corrigible—
then conclude: AI is good for society overall.

If, instead (in most deployments), AI commonly has the black-box/weak-accountability property such that ordinary users cannot understand, verify, or effectively challenge outputs, and this opacity makes critical-thinking erosion, racial discrimination, and privacy harms durable and hard to identify or reverse (locking in negative effects across contexts including employment and services), then conclude: AI is not reliably good for society overall.

If the evidence is mixed such that you cannot reliably establish either the multi-domain benefits-with-corrigibility condition or the durable black-box harms-with-lock-in condition, the conclusion remains unresolved.
